<p>A database change usually finishes after its SQL has finished. Adding a column makes room for a new value; it does not make existing rows correct, update every background job or stop an old application from writing the old format. A live change succeeds when the application, stored facts and recovery procedure agree on what the data now means.</p>
<p>Suppose the shop replaces an order’s free-text delivery address with separate address lines, town, postcode and country. Mina must still be able to edit an order while existing addresses are converted. A mistaken split could send a parcel to the wrong place, and some old addresses cannot be split reliably at all. We will follow that change from its first added column to removing the old text, then use the same reasoning to move a database.</p>
<p>The examples use an invented shop and illustrative schedules. PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete database behaviour; a small check used native PostgreSQL 18.6. The figures model decisions and outcomes, rather than running a production migration. Detailed schema operations, data jobs and moves have their own articles, linked at the stage where you need them.</p>

