<h2 id="a-change-has-more-than-one-finish-line">A change has more than one finish line</h2>
<p>Start by writing the rule the change must preserve: <strong>every order must retain a usable, current delivery address; a reader must never mistake unprocessed or stale parts for that address</strong>. During conversion, text is the source of truth. Structured parts are usable only after their meaning has been checked against the current source. Later, the parts become authoritative and the old text becomes a compatibility copy.</p>
<p>“Current” needs a way to recognise an address edit. Give the source an explicit version which every relevant writer advances. Record whether conversion is pending, needs review or is current, and which source version produced the parts. An empty second address line can be legitimate; it must not also mean “the worker has not reached this order”. The rule describes business meaning as well as column contents.</p>
<p>The change has several finish lines: the schema exists, writers maintain the transition rule, old rows are converted, readers use the new representation, and the old one can be retired. A <strong>schema migration record</strong> normally records that a particular database script ran. It cannot prove that a shipping worker was upgraded or an ambiguous address was resolved.</p>
<p>Figure 1 follows three existing orders through these finish lines. Step to <em>Backfill</em> and predict whether new readers may start. Then continue to <em>Reconcile</em>. The short addresses omit country and optional lines to keep the change visible.</p>
<ChangeFigure scene="evolution" number={1} />
<p>Order 103 stopped the read switch even though the other two rows were converted. Its text, “The old cottage, near York”, did not contain enough information. Customer confirmation supplied a precise address; the change did not invent a postcode. The job’s accepted count becomes useful evidence only alongside its unresolved cases and checks of meaning.</p>
<p>Give one change owner responsibility for the gates between stages. The owner needs the application and job inventory, transformation version, rehearsal results, measured limits and recovery decision. Each stage ends with recorded evidence and a named next action if that evidence is missing. A paused change can then be understood by someone who did not start it.</p>

<h2 id="keep-old-and-new-applications-compatible">Keep old and new applications compatible</h2>
<p>A rolling deployment leaves old and new application instances running together. The shop also has a shipping worker, an import task and queued edits created by earlier clients. Inventory all of them. An old client can remain supported if its request reaches a server that translates it through the new protocol; an old job that issues SQL directly is itself a writer that must change or be fenced.</p>
<p>Call the original application <strong>A</strong>: it reads and writes text. <strong>B</strong> is a bridge release that reads text and maintains the new representation. <strong>C</strong> reads the structured address. A complete sequence for this shop is:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Add the new schema.</strong> Keep the text column. Rehearse the operation and deploy it before code that needs its new columns. Test A against the expanded schema; positional inserts and unexpected result shapes can make even an addition incompatible.</li>
  <li><strong>Deploy B everywhere.</strong> B updates text, source version and the matching parts or explicit review state in one transaction. While A is draining, readers still use text and new parts are not trusted. Do not start the trusted backfill until old write transactions have finished and every writer follows B’s protocol.</li>
  <li><strong>Convert and reconcile existing rows.</strong> Protect each conversion against later edits. Resolve ambiguous addresses. Before the read switch, tighten every writer so an accepted address change must have confirmed, current parts; defer ambiguous requests to review rather than accepting a text-only change.</li>
  <li><strong>Deploy C to read parts.</strong> Verify ordinary reads, address edits and shipping. B and C still keep text current, so reverting C to the compatible B release remains possible during the agreed observation window.</li>
  <li><strong>Retire the text path.</strong> After B, old readers and delayed work are gone and the window has closed, stop maintaining text, then remove its column in a separate measured operation. Older releases are no longer valid rollback targets.</li>
</ol>
<p>Writing both representations is often called <strong>dual writing</strong>. The name supplies no guarantee. Two independent commits can leave half an edit. Two atomic writes can still commit mismatched values calculated from different source versions. Here one transaction derives both from the same accepted address; competing edits use the application’s concurrency protocol, and the backfill checks the source version it read. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops the atomicity and concurrency argument. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> handles request retries and uncertain commit outcomes.</p>
<p>A short maintenance window can be simpler. If the measured conversion and checks fit the allowed pause, stop admission, drain and fence writers, convert against stable data, deploy the compatible application, validate and reopen. Include failed validation and restart time in the rehearsal. The longer compatibility sequence earns its cost when the service cannot tolerate that pause; it adds versions and recovery states that somebody must operate.</p>

<h2 id="find-out-what-the-database-must-do">Find out what the database must do</h2>
<p>The logical change “add address fields” hides physical work. A database might update its <strong>catalogue</strong>, the definitions of its objects; scan every row to validate a rule; build an index; or rewrite rows and indexes into a new representation. The same-looking operation can take a different route with a different type, default, engine or version.</p>
<p>In PostgreSQL 18, adding ordinary nullable text columns without defaults does not require a table rewrite. It still needs an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock. A type change normally rewrites the table, subject to documented exceptions; validating a new constraint can scan it. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html">The PostgreSQL 18 ALTER TABLE reference</a> specifies the route and lock for each form. A quick catalogue change can therefore wait behind a long transaction before doing any useful work.</p>
<p>“Online” describes some permitted concurrency, not an impact budget. PostgreSQL’s concurrent index build permits writes but performs additional scans and waits, uses resources and can leave an invalid index after failure. It also cannot run inside a transaction block. A migration runner that wraps every script in a transaction changes what you can execute. Check both server and runner versions and the deployment’s restrictions against the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html">CREATE INDEX documentation</a>.</p>
<p>Before scheduling the work, inspect the table’s constraints, indexes, views, generated values, functions, triggers and replication consumers. Also inspect application queries, prepared statements and scheduled jobs: the database catalogue cannot discover every piece of external SQL. Decide which consumers need coordinated changes and which changes replication will carry.</p>
<p>Rehearse with representative volume, long transactions and concurrent traffic. Set separate lock-wait and execution limits, reserve storage for copies and logging, and record a workload budget. For example, an invented runbook might cancel a schema attempt after a one-second lock wait and pause a data job when the application’s agreed latency limit is exceeded. Those are decisions to measure for this service, not PostgreSQL recommendations. Name the signal, threshold, observation interval and person who can stop the work.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing schemas without breaking applications</a> turns these questions into PostgreSQL 18 procedures for constraints, indexes and a large representation change, including transaction boundaries and interrupted stages. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> to investigate unexpected work, and <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> for ongoing workload and replication signals.</p>

<h2 id="move-existing-data-while-writes-continue">Move existing data while writes continue</h2>
<p>A <strong>backfill</strong> supplies a new value for existing records. Adding columns left all three old orders unfinished. On a large table, one update of every order may hold locks and an open transaction for longer than the service can afford, produce too much log or overwhelm replicas. Smaller committed batches give the job places to pause, inspect its impact and resume.</p>
<p>A batch needs more than a row limit. Define its input scope, transformation version, how to identify a source version, how it records rejected or ambiguous inputs and how a restart finds unfinished work. New orders and edits must obey the same writer protocol. Commit changes and their durable progress together where possible; if the acknowledgement is lost, inspect that durable record before repeating the batch.</p>
<p>The worker must also avoid undoing an edit. Figure 2 starts with <em>Check source version</em>. Step through Mina changing order 101 after the worker reads it. At <em>Attempt</em>, switch to <em>Write without checking</em> and compare the stored parts. The town and postcode are unchanged and omitted here.</p>
<ChangeFigure scene="race" number={2} />
<p>The guarded write changed zero rows because version 7 was no longer current. That is a conflict to re-read or account for, not a successful conversion. A retry reads version 8 and uses its address. The blind write filled the column but restored the old street only on the new side. Switching readers would expose an error the text-based application had not shown.</p>
<p>For this single-row check, PostgreSQL’s Read Committed update semantics support a condition on the source version: after waiting for a competing updater, the condition is rechecked against its committed row. Every relevant writer must advance the version, and the worker must calculate from that guarded source. The native fixture checked the stale-version rejection; the figure is an illustrative interleaving. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html#XACT-READ-COMMITTED">PostgreSQL’s Read Committed documentation</a> defines the engine behaviour.</p>
<p>“Complete” means every in-scope source record has an accepted disposition, no required case remains unresolved, and accepted values match the current source and intended meaning. Equal row counts cannot prove that. A largest identifier is also not a commit boundary: a transaction may allocate a low identifier and commit after a traversal has passed it. Use an explicit enumeration and catch-up protocol, then reconcile while the writer protocol preserves the checked condition.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/data-changes/">Running reliable data changes</a> develops that complete job, including work coverage, atomic progress, bounded load and restart. It adapts the method for imports, whose input identities and duplicate policy need defining, and corrections, which must distinguish faulty values from later legitimate edits. Reusing ordinary application code also needs care: a data job must deliberately include or suppress effects such as emails and external events.</p>

<h2 id="switch-traffic-only-when-the-destination-is-ready">Switch traffic only when the destination is ready</h2>
<p>Moving the order database adds a second location for the same facts. Copying existing rows gives the destination yesterday’s state if orders keep changing. A move with a short final write pause needs a consistent starting copy joined to a change stream that carries later inserts, updates and deletes, with no gap. The capture tool must document that join, its restart position and how long changes remain available.</p>
<p>Before moving traffic, verify the destination’s schema and data meaning, constraints, identifiers, permissions, connections and representative application reads and writes. Test capacity under the intended workload. Check what the copy mechanism omits. PostgreSQL 18 logical replication, for example, does not replicate schema changes or sequence state; having all table rows does not establish that the next generated order identifier is safe. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html">Its documented restrictions</a> belong in the move’s checklist.</p>
<p>Figure 3 copies order 101 while order 102 is created. Continue through the pause and final checks, then admit order 103. Switch to <em>Change routing only</em> to see what an old source connection can still do. The change labels are illustrative boundaries, not replication measurements.</p>
<ChangeFigure scene="move" number={3} />
<p>Catching up at one instant did not stop the source accepting the next write. The safe cutover sequence is to stop admitting writes, drain or resolve in-flight requests, fence all source writers, record a final committed boundary, wait for the destination to apply it and complete final checks. Fencing means an enforced barrier against further source writes, covering jobs, administrators and old connection pools as well as the normal request route.</p>
<p>Only then refresh connections and admit destination writes, watching application results and workload limits. A final boundary is evidence about which committed changes arrived; a “lag is zero” display without that boundary is weaker evidence. If copying and validating everything fits the allowed write pause, a rehearsed fenced copy can avoid live change capture altogether.</p>
<p>Before the destination accepts writes, an abort can keep the source authoritative and reopen it after checks. Afterwards, the destination owns new orders that the source may lack. Returning needs a verified way to carry those writes back or another controlled move. Sending connections back alone would hide accepted orders. <a href="/review/relational-next/database-moves/">Moving databases with a controlled cutover</a> supplies the full ordered runbook and readiness evidence; <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">operations</a> owns the underlying replication and recovery procedures.</p>

<h2 id="plan-recovery-before-the-first-irreversible-step">Plan recovery before the first irreversible step</h2>
<p>“Roll back” can mean reverting application code, reversing a schema definition or undoing stored values. Those are different operations. Reverting C to B is safe only while B’s schema and current text still exist. Recreating a dropped column restores its name and type, not yesterday’s addresses.</p>
<p>Classify the stages before starting. For this change, the useful distinctions are:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Recovery choices during the address change"><table><thead><tr><th>Interruption</th><th>What survives</th><th>Safe next action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Backfill stops between batches</td><td>Committed values, dispositions and progress</td><td>Inspect the last durable state and resume with the same guarded transformation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>C’s read path fails during the observation window</td><td>Current parts and current compatibility text</td><td>Return to tested B; keep its writer protocol and investigate before retrying the read switch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A parser error is discovered</td><td>Source evidence and affected versions, if retained</td><td>Stop conversion and repair affected results conditionally; preserve later valid edits.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Text has been dropped or stopped being maintained</td><td>Only the new representation is reliably current</td><td>Repair forward, reconstruct with a verified transformation, or use the tested recovery route. A is no longer a rollback option.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A transformation that loses information cannot be reversed from its output alone. Retaining the original can make a correction possible, but blindly restoring it later can erase a valid edit. Keep enough identity, before-image and source-version evidence for the promised repair, under an agreed retention policy. An uncertain batch outcome first needs discovery of what committed, not an automatic inverse script.</p>
<p>Backups are part of this plan, not proof that every change is reversible. Restoring the whole database to before a bad conversion also leaves out valid orders accepted since then. Often the safer approach is to restore into an isolated database, extract the affected facts and apply a guarded correction to the live service. Establish the recovery time and reconciliation work in a rehearsal. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">point-in-time recovery documentation</a> describes recovery from a base backup and archived log; <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> develops the tested service procedure.</p>
<p>A stop condition must lead somewhere concrete. “Latency above the agreed limit for two observation intervals” might pause the worker after its current bounded transaction. A schema timeout requires inspection of the actual catalogue and committed stages. Missing final-boundary evidence aborts a move before destination writes. Record who takes that action and the evidence they retain, so a cancelled command cannot be confused with an undone change.</p>

<h2 id="retire-old-data-and-prove-the-change-is-finished">Retire old data and prove the change is finished</h2>
<p>Removing the text column is the last compatibility decision. First demonstrate that all old readers and writers have disappeared: deployed versions, database access, scheduled tasks and delayed queues must agree. Observation must cover the relevant job schedules and permitted client delay, or those paths must be explicitly disabled. Silence during a quiet hour does not prove that next week’s export has been upgraded.</p>
<p>Then require zero unexplained address mismatches, no unresolved required conversions, the intended constraints in place, representative shipping checks passing and a stable workload during the declared window. Remove obsolete code paths before dropping the schema they require. Keep the change record and repair evidence as long as their recovery purpose requires; temporary tables, privileged credentials and migration workers need an explicit cleanup owner.</p>
<p>A retention change follows the same method. Suppose the shop now removes delivery addresses from closed orders after an agreed period. Define “closed”, the time used for eligibility, exceptions and which related records lose the address. Include search copies, analytics extracts, replicas and exports in the inventory. Each has its own propagation or expiry guarantee; deleting the source row does not establish that every recoverable copy has disappeared.</p>
<p>Delete eligible data in bounded work, rechecking that the record still qualifies when it changes. Preserve relationships or deliberately transform them; a cascading deletion must match the retention decision. Check the effects on the live workload as with a backfill. In PostgreSQL, deleted row versions can remain until cleanup, and ordinary vacuum generally makes their space reusable inside the file rather than shrinking it immediately. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html#VACUUM-FOR-SPACE-RECOVERY">Routine Vacuuming</a> explains that storage distinction.</p>
<p>Backups may retain older addresses until their stated expiry. The restore procedure must apply the current retirement rules before restored data is returned to service, using the policy version or deletion record needed to identify what must stay retired. Decide whether an old backup is still a permitted recovery source under those guarantees. A claim about retention has to include restoration, not just today’s query results.</p>
<p>The address change can close with a short record: “B replaced all A writers; transformation v3 converted and reconciled the declared scope; ambiguous addresses were confirmed; C’s reads and shipping checks passed; new writes require current parts; the observation window passed; compatibility text was retired. C now owns delivery addresses. Repair evidence is retained until the recorded expiry; returning to A requires a new migration.” Attach the actual scope, dates, results, owners and recovery limits. That establishes what finished and what the next operator may safely rely on.</p>

